I started this experiment in rendering pixels to the screen because I notice that we are losing digital sovereignty(the ability to own and control our digital environment).

From my perspective, setting a simple color on the screen should be something easy. However, I feel it complicated, requiring the developer to jump through many hoops of modern graphics APIs. 

Fortunately, there is a countercurrent to this trend in independent projects such as [Serenum OS](https://samhsmith.com/serenum/), the famous [TempleOS](https://templeos.org/), [Pico-8](https://www.lexaloffle.com/pico-8.php), and many Linux-based operating systems.

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Vulkan vs OpenGL meme](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/vulkan_meme.png)
}

To take back some control, I want to build a simple software shader capable of drawing directly to the screen without relying on any graphics API. To achieve this, I used the *Linux framebuffer*, which provides a relatively direct way to manipulate pixels. It is not perfect, but it is close enough to the level of control I wanted to explore.

![Linux framebuffer demo](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/demo.mp4)

## Building a software shader from scratch

A shader can be defined as a function that takes pixel coordinates and returns a color.

$$
\text{shader}: (i, j) \to \text{color}
$$

@quote(){
Color in this article will be defined as a tuple from $(\red{\text{red}},\green{\text{green}}, \blue{\text{blue}}) \in [0,1]^3$ . Usually color is represented as a 32-bit integer, where each byte (8 bits) represents the intensity of red, green, blue, and alpha channels (<span style="color: red;">R</span>, <span style="color: green;">G</span>, <span style="color: blue;">B</span>, <span style="color: gray;">A</span>). But you can think of any other representation of color that you like. 
}

The screen is a grid of pixels indexed by coordinates $(i, j)$, where $i \in \{0, ..., H-1\}$ is the row, $j \in \{0, ..., W-1\}$ is the column, and the origin $(0, 0)$ is at the top-left corner.

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Pixel grid](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/pixel_grid.png)
}

To apply the shader, we iterate over every pixel and evaluate the shader at each coordinate.

```python
simple_shader(i, j):
    return (i / (H-1), j / (W-1), 0)  # (red, green, blue)

for i in {0, ..., H-1}:
    for j in {0, ..., W-1}:
        color = simple_shader(i, j)
        set_pixel(i, j, color)

```
We would get something like this:

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Simple shader output](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/simple_shader.png)
}

To make it more interesting, I suggest making a coordinate change from $(i,j) \rightarrow (x,y)$, where the coordinates start from the corner left of the screen and $x \in {0, ..., W-1}$ and $y \in {0, ..., H-1}$. These are the more natural coordinates to work with and are commonly used in graphics programming. 

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Coordinate change](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/coordinate_change.png)
}

```python
simple_shader(x, y):
    return (x / (W-1), y / (H-1), 0)

for i in {0, ..., H-1}:
    for j in {0, ..., W-1}:
        x = j
        y = H - 1 - i
        color = simple_shader(x, y)
        set_pixel(i, j, color)
```

The result is:

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Simple shader with coordinate change](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/simple_shader_coordinate_change.png)
}

This is very cool and all, but the computer does not actually store a grid of pixels, instead, it stores a contiguous block of memory.

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Actual framebuffer memory layout - simplified](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/memory_layout.png)
}

To solve this issue, we need to iterate over the memory instead of pixel coordinates. But for that we need a way to calculate the corresponding pixel coordinates from the memory index. Let's see a simple example:

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Simple grid example](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/simple_combinatory.png)
}

As the image above suggests, $j$ index the columns and we need to jump $W$ columns for each row $i$. So we can calculate the memory address as $k = i \cdot W + j$. For a generic case, check the image below:

@scaleDiv(66){
    ![Combinatorial diagram to calculate pixel coordinates](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/combinatorial_diagram.png)
}

Now to compute the pixel coordinates from the memory address, we must reverse the formula. It seems impossible, because we have one equation (for $k$) and two unknowns ($i$ and $j$). But we can note the following statement from regular arithmetic:

$$
N, d, q, r \in \mathbb{N}, \quad N = q \cdot d + r, \qquad q = \left\lfloor\frac{N}{d}\right\rfloor, \quad r = N \bmod d, \quad 0 \le r < d
$$

Where $N$ is the number we want to divide, $d$ is the divisor, $q$ is the quotient, and $r$ is the remainder. By pattern matching the formula $k = i \cdot W + j$ with the statement above, we can find the solution for $i$ and $j$:
$$
\red{N} = \blue{q} \cdot \green{d} + \orange{r} \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \red{k} = \blue{i} \cdot \green{W} + \orange{j}
$$

Hence:
$$
\blue{i} = \left\lfloor\frac{\red{k}}{\green{W}}\right\rfloor, \quad \orange{j} = \red{k} \bmod \green{W}
$$

Putting everything together, we can render the shader to the screen with the following code:

```python
simple_shader(x, y):
    return (x / (W-1), y / (H-1), 0)

for k in {0, ..., W*H-1}:
    i = k % W
    j = floor(k / W)
    x = j
    y = H - 1 - i
    color = simple_shader(x, y)
    screen_memory[k] = color
```

To make things even more interesting, we can add a time variable to the shader, which will allow us to create animations. The demo animation above is generated by the following shader:

```python
simple_shader(x, y, t):
    r = ((x / (W-1)) * t) % 1
    g = ((y / (H-1)) * t) % 1
    return (r, g, 0)

old_time = measure_time_in_seconds()
time = 0
while time < 10:
    current_time = measure_time_in_seconds()
    dt = current_time - old_time
    old_time = current_time
    time += dt
    for k in {0, ..., W*H-1}:
        i = k % W
        j = floor(k / W)
        x = j
        y = H - 1 - i
        color = simple_shader(x, y, time)
        screen_memory[k] = color

```

@details("Clean up version of the demo") {
    ![Clean demo](/posts/LinuxFrameBuffer/simple_animation_shader.mp4)
}


